{"name":"Seashell Peach","hex":"#fff6de","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"seashell","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The empty shell of a marine mollusk.","The animal living inside a seashell."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a very light pink colour, like that of some seashells."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seashell"},{"term":"peach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tree of species Prunus persica, native to China and now widely cultivated throughout temperate regions, having pink flowers and edible fruit.","Soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the color peach.","Particularly pleasing or agreeable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Seashell","blocks":[{"text":"A seashell, also known simply as a shell, is a hard, protective outer layer usually created by an animal or organism that lives in the sea. Most seashells are made by mollusks, such as snails, clams, and oysters to protect their soft insides. Empty seashells are often found washed up on beaches by beachcombers. The shells are empty because the animal has died and the soft parts have decomposed or been eaten by another organism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Conchiglie_Seashells_01.jpg/330px-Conchiglie_Seashells_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seashell"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach"},{"title":"Seashell (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Seashell is an off-white color that resembles some of the very pale pinkish tones that are common in many seashells."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/White_ribbed_Anadara_valve_on_sand_in_Venezuela.jpg/330px-White_ribbed_Anadara_valve_on_sand_in_Venezuela.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seashell_(color)"},{"title":"Peach (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Peach is a color that is named for the pale color of the interior flesh of the peach fruit. Like the color apricot, the color peach is paler than most actual peach fruits and seems to have been formulated primarily to create a pastel palette of colors for interior design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Shell_Island_1985.jpg/500px-Shell_Island_1985.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Shell_Island_1985.jpg/1280px-Shell_Island_1985.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Shell_Island_1985.jpg/500px-Shell_Island_1985.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShell_Island_1985.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Selection_of_seashells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Selection_of_seashells.jp